WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.420
Hello, John

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.340
By now, you will have received my message that we will no longer be communicating through any texts or means.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.360
No more instant messaging, no more emailing.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.420
Only video blogging.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.200
You can see my eye in my eye...

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:27.600
Ahhh

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.860
Okay, let's try to ignore that.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.660
There. You can't see it now, can you?

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.460
Last time I sent you an email from a New Year's Eve party in Lake Tahoe

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.460
We emailed all our plans.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.340
Starting on January 1st, today, I will send you a video blog.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.920
Tomorrow, you will reply to that video blog.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.180
We will continue like this until the year is up.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.720
If one of us fails to send a video blog on a weekday, there will be certain punishments.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.531
Brotherhood 2.0 commences today.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:03.969
Good morning, Hank. It is January 2nd, 2007.

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:07.589
I've just spent 2 hours and 13 minutes downloading your two minutes and one second video,

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.720
which can only mean one thing:

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.850
that I'm at Mom and Dad's house --

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:16.590
the last residence in the United States of America with dial-up internet.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.420
On the upside, it's awfully pretty.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:22.250
Plus I get to be surrounded by memories of our childhood.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.920
I know we've already agreed to dole out punishments if one of us fails to update our video blog on the appointed date.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.780
I've been thinking that we could also periodically give each other challenges.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.860
I've come up with a few suggested challenges:

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.520
We-eat-five-sheets-of-toilet-paper-while-discussing-the-political-situation-in-Nepal challenge

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.700
(whispers) I'm very interested in political situation in Nepal

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.600
We-drink-a-glass-of-strawberry-hill-wine-without-hurling challenge

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.460
Maybe we can make Thursdays and Fridays challenge days?

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:48.270
Hi John,

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.750
(heavy breathing)

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.170
good morning!

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:54.600
Wow, that guy was having fun.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.820
It's seven o'clock in the morning and I'm about to get on a plane.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.060
Yesterday, I was sledding down the hill at Lake Tahoe.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:02.380
That was fun.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:03.790
This isn't fun.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.160
I hope you don't mind if I make a suggestion as to how to begin

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:07.910
our conversation on this video blog.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.520
Being your brother is kind of frustrating sometimes.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:13.579
This is because people walk up to me and they say,

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.200
"Gosh, you look so much like your brother!"

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.200
Apparently, some people even think that we look like the twin rock duo, The Proclaimers.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.960
Which is admittedly better than people thinking that we look a twin rock duo, Nelson.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.060
So, I would like to propose a list:

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.820
we make a video blog list of all the ways

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.700
in which we are very, very different people.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.600
Things like, in the last few years,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.340
John Green has gotten kind of pudgy.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:42.840
Whereas, in the next few years, Hank Green will get kind of pudgy

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:44.340
And also things like,

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:49.000
John Green has never spilled a tiny amount of beer on his wife's MacBook...

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.340
which then proceeded to not boot up.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.240
And also, John Green didn't do the happy dance when his wife was in the shower

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.580
and he reinstalled the battery and tried to boot it up the next morning,

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:02.920
and it worked.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.060
*Hank dances aggressively*

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.700
Good morning Hank, it's Thursday, January fourth.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.740
I'm standing on top of Little Pisgah Mountain in North Carolina.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:13.580
Hank, in your last video,

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.440
you asked me to continue our discussion of our differences.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:17.400
I think one of the most

00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:19.090
important differences between us is that

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.459
you are excellent at living in a way

00:03:21.459 --> 00:03:24.779
that's commensurate with your values, whereas, I am not.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.980
For instance, I didn't recycle until I watched "The Inconvenient Truth"

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:30.360
and I'm still sort of iffy on it.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:36.340
And also... uh, I didn't vote in 2000

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.600
even though I could have voted in Florida.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.000
(hitting himself) Argh, George Bush! It's all my fault, argh! So stupid!

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.280
(large sigh)

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:47.840
Let's change the subject.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.700
Also, we have vastly different happy dances.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.720
(John demonstrates his happy dance)

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:55.660
Yes!

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:00.100
What I noticed that in your last video is you completely ignored my

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.880
eat-five-sheets-of-toilet-paper-while-discussing-the-political-situation-in-Nepal challenge.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.580
And if you're not going to eat five

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:06.780
sheets of toilet paper while discussing

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:09.190
the political situation in Nepal...

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:11.230
Well, I'm going to.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:14.340
(disgusted noise)

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.220
The thing about the political situation in Nepal

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.500
is that there are these Maoist rebels who can hold

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:25.920
one half of the country.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:28.620
Who has Maoist rebels anymore?

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:29.900
(disgusted) Oh

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:31.560
No

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:33.160
(coughing noises)

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.320
I can't do it, Hank.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.160
I think this stuff is 2-ply, too.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.699
I think that was part of the problem, because you

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:42.909
get the 1-ply stuff, I can just --(eating noises)-- shoot down that stuff,

00:04:42.909 --> 00:04:45.020
but this stuff? Ooh.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.419
Good morning John, it's January fifth,

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:47.919
and I'm sitting in my basement,

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.700
back home, surrounded by all the things that I love.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.060
I love orange juice.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:58.060
I love Mental Floss magazine and Benjamin Franklin.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.800
I love that (points) right there. See it? There.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:06.280
That's a map of the realm of Narnia.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.060
Hold on, I have to go upstairs and get something.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.380
What did I get?

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:14.080
We don't actually have Strawberry Hill here.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.120
I didn't know that.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:17.680
I'd never look for it.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:20.320
Sutter Home's White Zinfandel have to do.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:21.640
(cork pops off)

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.580
A commentary suggested that I drink a glass of Strawberry Hill wine

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:25.940
and then say:

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:29.240
"Eat five sheets of toilet paper while discussing the political situation in Nepal"

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:30.760
really fast,

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:31.880
without vomiting.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:33.720
I'm going to do that.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.640
And there's only one way that I'm going to be able to do that...

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.240
and that's by eating corn dogs.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:45.260
Eating corndogs is the only way I can chug an alcoholic beverage without puking.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:46.840
I don't know why.

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:49.080
I think, maybe, my stomach's just like:

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.420
"Ohh, that corn dog is so good, I just can't let go of it!"

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:04.720
Not Strawberry Hill, but it only cost three dollars and sixty-five cents.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:06.080
(sniffs) Eugh.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:09.260
To Brotherhood.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:13.240
(gulping)

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:23.080
Eat five sheets of toilet paper while discussing the political situation in Nepal.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:24.780
(victory grunt)

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:26.620
(sing-song like) Eat

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:27.720
five sheets

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.540
of toilet paper while discussing the political situation in Nepal.

